@page "/docs/registry/repositories"
<PageTitle>Registry repositories - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Repositories</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">Registry</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">nothing to host</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p class="max-w-3xl">A public repository can be a registry as it stands: commit a <code>registry.json</code> next to your components and consumers install from <code>owner/repo/item</code>. No build step, no host, no namespace to record.</p>
    </header>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Installing</h2>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/Repository" />
        <p>The first two segments are the repository; the third is an item <em>name</em> from its manifest, never a file path. Everything after <code>#</code> is a git ref: a branch, a tag, or a full commit. Without one, the repository's default branch is read, so what you install can change between runs; pin a tag or a commit for anything you depend on.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">What is read</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><b><code>registry.json</code> at the repository root.</b> The same manifest documented on the <a href="docs/registry/registry-json">manifest reference</a>, <code>include</code> and all.</li>
            <li><b>The source files it names</b>, fetched from the same ref as the manifest, so a pinned address stays pinned all the way down.</li>
            <li><b>Nothing else.</b> There is no built output to read, so a repository ships plain source: no per-skin variants, and any <code>content</code> committed into the manifest is re-read from the file itself.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Publishing this way</h2>
        <p>Commit the manifest at the root, keep <CmdChip Command="blaizio registry validate" /> in CI, and tag your releases. That is the whole pipeline: consumers install from tags, and the repository is the distribution.</p>
        <p>Build and host the output instead when you want per-skin variants, a catalogue that costs one request, or a private registry. Those are the <a href="docs/registry/getting-started">hosted</a> and <a href="docs/registry/authentication">authenticated</a> paths, and a registry can be published both ways from one repository.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Limits</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Public repositories only.</b> A private repository answers the same way a missing one does, and there is no token to send. A repository behind credentials belongs behind a <a href="docs/registry/authentication">namespace with headers</a>.</li>
            <li><b>GitHub.</b> Other hosts are not addressed this way yet; they can still serve a built registry over a URL.</li>
            <li><b>One request per file.</b> A repository has no catalogue to read in one go, so a large install is chattier than a hosted registry.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Trust</h2>
        <p>A repository address is a stranger's source code, so it goes through the same gate a direct URL does: the CLI warns and asks before the first install from a repository it has not seen approved, and records the approval per REPOSITORY (<code>https://github.com/owner/repo</code>), not per host. Approving one project on GitHub approves exactly that project.</p>
        <p>Read before you accept: <CmdChip Command="blaizio view" /> prints every file the item carries without writing anything, and <code>--dry-run</code> shows the files, targets, packages and sibling items an install would bring. Pinning a full commit makes what you reviewed the thing you install. More on the <a href="docs/registry/trust">trust page</a>.</p>
    </section>
</div>
